<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ghassanids</h1><p class="st-meta">502 CE – 638 CE (approx.) · capital Jabiyah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Christian Arab kings who guarded Byzantium's desert frontier for over a century and patronised the Miaphysite church in Syria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ghassanid-kingdom">Ghassanids</a> were part of the Azd, a tribal group that migrated north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>. Entering Roman territory in the late 5th century, they defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salihids</a> and around 502 made a treaty with the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus">Anastasius</a> that made them the empire's chief Arab allies in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Justinian made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-harith-ibn-jabala">al-Harith ibn Jabala</a> supreme phylarch and king over all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> Arab allies around 529 to counter the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia's</a> clients; he killed the Lakhmid king al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mundhir-iii">Mundhir III</a> in battle in 554. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ghassanid-kingdom">Ghassanids</a> moved between camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabiyah">Jabiyah</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan</a>, built churches and towers, and fervently supported Miaphysite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, obtaining the consecration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-baradaeus">Jacob Baradaeus</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Their court attracted Arabic poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nabigha-al-dhubyani">al-Nabigha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-thabit">Hassan ibn Thabit</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 581 the emperor Maurice arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mundhir-ibn-al-harith">al-Mundhir ibn al-Harith</a> and exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and the confederation fragmented. Partly revived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ghassanid-kingdom">Ghassanids</a> fought for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-yarmuk">Yarmuk</a> in 636; afterwards some accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and others, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabala-ibn-al-ayham">Jabala ibn al-Ayham</a>, withdrew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ghassanids.jpg" alt="Ghassanids" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: MoeyElan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghassanid_Influence_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>502 CE – 638 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jabiyah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>al-Harith ibn Jabala, al-Mundhir ibn al-Harith, Jabala ibn al-Ayham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Miaphysite Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a>, <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ghassanid Kingdom, Banu Ghassan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=570&amp;focus=ghassanids" aria-label="Ghassanids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5602 979 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ghassanids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5922 1235L5709 970M5922 1235L6053 1533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5922 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5922 1235h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jabiyah. <a href="/atlas?year=570&amp;focus=ghassanids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
